794
01:07:58,480 --> 01:08:09,600
And that's why I kind of didn't feel the need to like turn or to force the money question before I felt like I had enough of a value prop to do it.

795
01:08:10,320 --> 01:08:14,560
We also did a like a one point zero release that introduced a bunch of new features.

796
01:08:14,880 --> 01:08:22,480
So it wasn't like end of beta and now we just like switched this, it was we introduced like ten new features in the one point zero release.

797
01:08:23,200 --> 01:08:26,240
That was the calendar integration stuff, right?

798
01:08:26,400 --> 01:08:27,120
Was that with the calendar?

799
01:08:27,200 --> 01:08:35,680
Yeah, we added like multiple count profiles, we added support for server side filters and just a whole bunch of other things involved too.

800
01:08:36,160 --> 01:08:37,520
I'm kind of forgetting some of them now.

801
01:08:37,600 --> 01:08:38,800
Now it's been a little while.

802
01:08:38,960 --> 01:08:40,880
I've just gotten used to them being part of the app.

803
01:08:41,760 --> 01:08:50,080
But yeah, certainly filters and profiles were like the two headline, two most headline features that we added in the one.

804
01:08:50,400 --> 01:08:50,480
zero.

805
01:08:51,040 --> 01:09:02,080
Yeah, so profiles are the, you know, if you've used the mail app or any email client that lets you use multiple accounts, it's like you have, you know, your left sidebar, you can have all your different email accounts.

806
01:09:02,160 --> 01:09:03,200
You can see them all unified.

807
01:09:04,080 --> 01:09:11,840
But then profiles let you kind of group those email accounts into like I have a work one and a personal one.

808
01:09:12,480 --> 01:09:14,640
And so it's kind of like a way to.

809
01:09:14,880 --> 01:09:22,640
to group your different email clients or sorry, your email addresses into separate, like, sort of chunks.

810
01:09:23,040 --> 01:09:23,840
Is that an accurate way?

811
01:09:24,720 --> 01:09:25,280
Yeah, exactly.

812
01:09:25,440 --> 01:09:33,360
And then you can have all your personal accounts still be shown as one unified inbox and all your work accounts still showed as one unified inbox if that's something that you like.

813
01:09:34,320 --> 01:09:42,240
And we thought that was kind of like the best of both worlds in terms of unified inbox versus accounts separate because that was always, you know, some people love unified inbox.

814
01:09:42,400 --> 01:09:45,280
Some people were just like, I don't want my personal and work email mixed together.

815
01:09:45,440 --> 01:09:46,560
We want it totally separate.

816
01:09:47,680 --> 01:10:01,360
And then we did some other integrations with the system, like being able, not be interrupted by work notifications, you know, on the weekend and also integrating with, like, the system focus state.

817
01:10:01,680 --> 01:10:06,800
So when you enter a certain focus, you can turn off your, you know, work e mails when you're in a certain focus.

818
01:10:06,880 --> 01:10:09,760
You can turn off your personal e mails when you're in the work focus, et cetera.

819
01:10:11,280 --> 01:10:18,480
So there's a couple of different kind of nice integrations in there to help you kind of focus and manage the distraction of e mail.

820
01:10:19,680 --> 01:10:20,960
So that launch went out.

821
01:10:21,440 --> 01:10:22,960
What was the, like, reaction?

822
01:10:23,200 --> 01:10:24,080
Like, how did that all go?

823
01:10:25,280 --> 01:10:27,840
The reaction was, you know, kind of what you would expect.

824
01:10:28,160 --> 01:10:45,120
I would say that fortunately, the move of consumer productivity apps from onetime payments to subscriptions had fortunately already been a well trotted path by the time we came out.

825
01:10:46,080 --> 01:10:57,600
And so it wasn't, we certainly heard people that were not seeing the value in paying for, for, for, you know, a consumer email app.

826
01:10:57,840 --> 01:10:58,960
And I totally understand that.

827
01:10:59,920 --> 01:11:04,960
And, you know, like, if that's if your requirements don't call for it then why should you pay?

828
01:11:05,120 --> 01:11:06,560
That's, that's perfectly fair..

829
01:11:08,160 --> 01:11:10,480
But on the whole it was relatively positive.

830
01:11:11,760 --> 01:11:20,240
You know, honestly, the people, the number of beta users that converted into paying users was a lot, a lot higher than I was anticipating.

831
01:11:20,560 --> 01:11:21,440
Oh, that's nice.

832
01:11:21,760 --> 01:11:26,640
And yeah, that was, I mean, it was like three times higher than I was anticipating.

833
01:11:27,680 --> 01:11:27,920
Dang.

834
01:11:28,080 --> 01:11:29,360
I was like, that's great.

835
01:11:30,320 --> 01:11:32,400
At least the people that used it every single day.

836
01:11:33,200 --> 01:11:41,840
Now, the people that you only opened it like once a week and then didn't open it every single day, those people kind of like, you know, obviously that segment mostly dropped off.

837
01:11:42,000 --> 01:11:42,160
Right.

838
01:11:42,880 --> 01:11:50,000
But the people that had it open every day, the majority, stuck with the app.

839
01:11:50,480 --> 01:11:50,640
Dang.

840
01:11:51,040 --> 01:11:54,000
Which is, yeah, just pretty good for a consumer app.

841
01:11:54,800 --> 01:12:11,600
New users are a little harder to sell to when you have, it's sort of like, you know, a price up front and you kind of don't really let people, like, kind of get super familiar with the app and like why they should use it.

842
01:12:12,880 --> 01:12:15,760
And is, you know, we currently do a 14 day free trial.

843
01:12:15,920 --> 01:12:16,880
So that's a good question.

844
01:12:16,960 --> 01:12:17,040
Yeah.

845
01:12:17,520 --> 01:12:19,120
We take a little bit of a spin on the free trial.

846
01:12:19,200 --> 01:12:21,280
Like, we don't autom automatically convert or anything.

847
01:12:21,440 --> 01:12:22,960
We don't ask for a credit card in advance.

848
01:12:23,040 --> 01:12:25,200
We just let you download the app and just start using it.

849
01:12:25,360 --> 01:12:32,000
And just like at the end of the fourteen days, if you're convinced, then you can enter your credit card.

850
01:12:32,320 --> 01:12:35,200
Which is easier to do because you're outside of the app store.

851
01:12:35,280 --> 01:12:37,600
Yeah, you're not in the Mac App store at all, right?

852
01:12:37,680 --> 01:12:39,760
No, we're not in the Mac App store.

853
01:12:40,720 --> 01:12:44,240
And, you know, we'll eventually get there when we launch our iOS app.

854
01:12:45,280 --> 01:12:47,680
But for now, we're outside.

855
01:12:48,160 --> 01:13:01,920
And that lets us do a couple of things that, you know, we can get out of our sandbox with a little helper agent for a couple of things that are just, it allows us to deliver a little bit of a better experience for certain things.

856
01:13:03,360 --> 01:13:06,720
The Mac version, the Mac App Store version would just have that all snipped out.

857
01:13:06,880 --> 01:13:10,480
Like we'd still offer it, but there would be a few little things here and there that are snipped out.

858
01:13:11,360 --> 01:13:16,400
We also do a fair amount of business licensing.

859
01:13:17,200 --> 01:13:31,200
And so this is like being able to sell volume licenses to a business and letting anyone with a custom domain at that business's domain activate and use the app.

860
01:13:31,840 --> 01:13:33,520
Was that, that wasn't there at launch, was it?

861
01:13:34,080 --> 01:13:34,480
It was there.

862
01:13:34,720 --> 01:13:36,960
It was that was there at launch.

863
01:13:37,120 --> 01:13:45,200
And that was, honestly, the key reason why we didn't just go with the App Store subscription infrastructure, uh, was to be able to, to do that.

864
01:13:46,080 --> 01:13:52,080
Um, and, uh, yeah, that has been surprisingly, um, that has been surprisingly positive.

865
01:13:52,480 --> 01:14:07,120
Uh, there's a lot of, you know, security and privacy reasons why an IT admin might prefer to basically shut off all IMAP access and only specifically allow this, this one app to use the Gmail API.

866
01:14:07,760 --> 01:14:14,400
Uh, and, you know, we have some additional features for management of, you know, company licenses, et cetera.

867
01:14:15,120 --> 01:14:20,480
But that was that was the key reason that, you know, we can have just a group licensing infrastructure.

868
01:14:20,560 --> 01:14:25,840
It's just not there on the App Store, unless you do the like traditional onetime purchase for their volume purchase program.

869
01:14:27,200 --> 01:14:30,000
But yeah, so on the whole, it went super well.

870
01:14:30,320 --> 01:14:40,080
Obviously, if you launch any software product with a subscription, you're going to see some people that are going to write comments in articles or write comments on forums.

871
01:14:40,880 --> 01:14:44,480
That should be expected by anyone that decides to go that way.

872
01:14:45,600 --> 01:15:12,240
But I mean, I think it's I think it's pretty well establblished now that if you are, if your goal is to build a best of class app that is continuously investing in polish, continuously fixing bugs that are reported, continuously adding features and functionality, continuously adopting the newest features of the OS releases that are released every year, how could you not do a subscription?

873
01:15:12,400 --> 01:15:16,880
There's just that's the obvious kind of licensing model this day and age.

874
01:15:17,520 --> 01:15:22,240
It's amazing how much stuff breaks every time there's a new OS release.

875
01:15:22,400 --> 01:15:30,080
And I'm not even talking about major O even the minor more and more updates can really kill the app.

876
01:15:30,400 --> 01:15:35,280
I mean, we've had, I remember it was like macOS 12.3.

877
01:15:36,720 --> 01:15:45,520
There was some minor timing change in some APIs that caused basically all emails to send blank from the timestream data.

878
01:15:46,400 --> 01:15:58,160
And that was, that was the experience where I was like, oh, you know, for a long time I did seriously, I did seriously consider selling version licenses for the app.

879
01:15:58,880 --> 01:16:02,320
And truthfully, we built out all the licensing infrastructureture.

880
01:16:02,560 --> 01:16:02,960
Oh, wow.

881
01:16:03,200 --> 01:16:05,360
To sell versioned licenses as well.

882
01:16:06,160 --> 01:16:07,520
Uh, so we built that all.

883
01:16:07,760 --> 01:16:11,680
That was another reason why we didn't necessarily go with the App Store was.

884
01:16:12,000 --> 01:16:13,920
Are you thinking maybe we'll sell?

885
01:16:14,960 --> 01:16:18,480
No, just for the users that just really don't like a subscription.

886
01:16:18,960 --> 01:16:37,920
Um, so I thought about doing that and then macOS 12.3, you know, killing the like completely killing the sent messages that just made me feel like, uh, yeah, because the old expectation of that would have been you still do, uh, like patches and stuff for those old versions.

887
01:16:38,000 --> 01:16:50,480
So then you do like, yeah, every year, every, not even every year, every point release, what, you have to run a big suite of tests against every version of your app that you've sold licenses for.

888
01:16:50,720 --> 01:17:07,760
It just seems and it's also difficult to compare like legacy versions of Adobe software or legacy versions of Microsoft software that might be versioned against like a relatively new indie app because internally, I mean, they have a list of must not break apps.

889
01:17:08,560 --> 01:17:18,000
You know, the like frameworks are littered with one-off fixes for weakird expectations of old apps.

890
01:17:18,240 --> 01:17:23,360
But a new, like a small indie app like, you know, MimeStream would not benefit from that kind of treatment.

891
01:17:23,600 --> 01:17:30,480
So the, the, you know, people are like, oh, I can run my version of Microsoft Office from eight years ago, nine years ago.

892
01:17:30,720 --> 01:17:38,000
It's like, well, that wouldn't apply to a much smaller app because that, that, that app is running because Apple is making sure it's still running.

893
01:17:38,160 --> 01:17:38,240
Yeah.

894
01:17:38,720 --> 01:17:41,280
It's not running just because it's just an accident.

895
01:17:42,000 --> 01:17:43,280
Um, so there was that.

896
01:17:43,520 --> 01:17:49,360
We have also had like Gmail API changes that also just caused the app to start crashing on launch instantly.

897
01:17:49,520 --> 01:17:54,880
In fact, I had to send out an embarrassing email saying, I'm sorry guys, all your apps are crashing on launch.

898
01:17:55,280 --> 01:17:56,960
Here's a link to the updated version.

899
01:17:57,520 --> 01:18:00,800
We can't even install the update because it crashes as soon as you try it.

900
01:18:00,880 --> 01:18:02,880
I think I remember that install the update.

901
01:18:03,120 --> 01:18:03,200
Yeah.

902
01:18:03,680 --> 01:18:22,800
So, uh, you know, and there's several other like less catastrophic versions of that, but by the end of the beta, um, I became fairly convinced that it just, it wasn't going to be fair to sell, uh, a one versioned license to users.

903
01:18:23,040 --> 01:18:29,040
I think that became really clear to me that like the expectation of people was like, oh, you know, I'll buy this app.

904
01:18:29,680 --> 01:18:33,200
and it will work forever with the features that I paid for.

905
01:18:34,400 --> 01:18:45,600
And that would have been their expectation around a version license and just cutting it off and no longer sending updates to that was just not going to be feasible because it was just going to be dead in the water within a year anyway.

906
01:18:46,080 --> 01:18:49,600
On either end, on the platform, on either end, on the Mac or Gmail.

907
01:18:50,160 --> 01:18:50,320
Right.

908
01:18:50,560 --> 01:18:57,360
So either we really had to sell a real lifetime subscription or subscription only.

909
01:18:58,000 --> 01:19:05,120
And the real lifetime thing just, I mean, my goal was not I'm not trying to do this for two or three years, get in, get out.

910
01:19:05,360 --> 01:19:05,600
Right?

911
01:19:06,000 --> 01:19:26,720
I mean, my goal is to be able to be like, okay, and in, you know, be it five years, ten years from now, I want MimeStream to be like totally on top as, you know, supporting all the major services, supporting lots of platforms, just be the clear best prosumer email client.

912
01:19:26,880 --> 01:19:35,840
If that's if you've got email needs, MimeStream's where you come and you get a good email client supports all your services, every, every device and platform that you have.

913
01:19:37,200 --> 01:19:41,120
And I just don't see how to get that with, you know, a lifetime payment.

914
01:19:41,440 --> 01:19:49,040
You can make some arguments about, like, you know, the lifetime value of a customer versus the payment and that tradeoff.

915
01:19:50,800 --> 01:19:55,840
And maybe, you know, that's something that, you know, could arguably be worth revisiting.

916
01:19:56,000 --> 01:19:59,200
I know a lot of apps have had some success with doing that.

917
01:19:59,680 --> 01:20:06,080
I feel like your attention numbers are going to be very different than a normal consumer subscription mobile app, right?

918
01:20:06,240 --> 01:20:06,880
Where it's like, right.

919
01:20:06,960 --> 01:20:12,080
Where it's like, what percentage of your users are still actively using your app three years later or something.

920
01:20:12,400 --> 01:20:13,520
But it's like, right.

921
01:20:13,920 --> 01:20:22,560
If Mime stream continues the way, like, it's going to be like Fantastic Alice for so many people, where it's like, how many of those users are have been using it for a decade plus?

922
01:20:22,880 --> 01:20:23,040
Right.

923
01:20:23,200 --> 01:20:23,680
You know what I mean?

924
01:20:23,840 --> 01:20:32,400
Like, and also it was, it's like when you test paywalls, you like very quickly learn that the more options you add, the more like confusion and drop off you get.

925
01:20:32,880 --> 01:20:36,000
So you just don't really want to add a lot of unnecessary options.

926
01:20:36,640 --> 01:20:37,840
You want to keep it like simple.

927
01:20:38,320 --> 01:20:40,240
Yeah, pitman paywalls are weird.

928
01:20:40,560 --> 01:20:44,240
Like I'm literally testing one right now, which is doing the opposite of that.

929
01:20:44,400 --> 01:20:47,920
And I'm, my numbers are low enough that it's not significant.

930
01:20:48,080 --> 01:20:52,880
But I've heard this where showing more options can actually convert better, weirdly.

931
01:20:53,200 --> 01:20:57,360
But everything with paywalls seems to behave different than what my intuition would say.

932
01:20:58,400 --> 01:21:03,920
But that's on the mobile, very consumer, not prosumer level.

933
01:21:04,160 --> 01:21:12,080
So when you say showing more options, do you mean showing fewer options or do you mean showing because my experience was showing fewer options was converting better than.

934
01:21:12,800 --> 01:21:15,280
So I've heard this from a lot of people, which is why I'm running it.

935
01:21:15,600 --> 01:21:17,280
It also depends on the app and space.

936
01:21:17,920 --> 01:21:18,240
Exactly.

937
01:21:18,400 --> 01:21:18,560
Right.

938
01:21:18,720 --> 01:21:19,280
It's so different.

939
01:21:19,520 --> 01:21:32,960
But yeah, I've I've heard from a lot of people that if so I have like, you know, one annual plan or whatever, but I also do have a lifetime and a monthly, but I kind of had it hidden behind a like all plans button.

940
01:21:33,120 --> 01:21:34,320
So it's kind of like I'm not.

941
01:21:34,320 --> 01:21:39,600
It's kinda like I'm not making anyone make a choice, but if they were going to bounce, they could find that and see whatever.

942
01:21:41,040 --> 01:21:50,560
But I've heard from so many people that showing all the plans upfront actually performs better that I was like, I'll run an experiment and try this.

943
01:21:51,520 --> 01:22:07,920
And again, I'm early enough in the experiment and my numbers aren't gigantic, so it's not statistically significant, but I think when I checked this morning, it was like, you know, like a ten or fifteen percent better result on the one showing all the plans versus the one showing only one.

944
01:22:08,320 --> 01:22:08,480
Right.

945
01:22:08,800 --> 01:22:11,200
Which is like, it's like the same for onboarding, like I've heard.

946
01:22:11,920 --> 01:22:19,360
really long onboarding flows perform better than short, quick get the person into the app quickly.

947
01:22:19,680 --> 01:22:26,240
I think it has something to do with like your signaling value.

948
01:22:26,720 --> 01:22:29,920
Like the longer that onboarding is, the more it's like, oh, this thing does a lot.

949
01:22:30,400 --> 01:22:39,040
Like it's not a simple basic thing, which from a product perspective, especially kind of having the Apple ethos, we're always thinking in terms of progressive onboarding.

950
01:22:39,200 --> 01:22:44,480
You want somebody to open the app and be like, this is so simple and it does exactly what they need and that's it.

951
01:22:44,720 --> 01:22:49,360
And then every time you want something more, you like, discover it, which is great.

952
01:22:49,600 --> 01:22:49,760
Exactly.

953
01:22:50,080 --> 01:22:58,960
But if you're trying to sell someone on a subscription, you want to signal to them at the very beginning that, like, this does so much stuff.

954
01:22:59,200 --> 01:23:02,160
So, I don't know, it's kind of paywalls are weird, is basically.

955
01:23:02,720 --> 01:23:05,040
Yeah, I've been struggling with that exact question myself.

956
01:23:05,200 --> 01:23:10,560
You know, MimeStream has a fairly light onboarding of just kind of getting you to set up an account and get right in.

957
01:23:10,800 --> 01:23:14,480
But then, you know, I do hear people being like, I opened it.

958
01:23:15,120 --> 01:23:16,080
What does it do different?

959
01:23:16,560 --> 01:23:20,560
And it's like, well, we probably should maybe get into like a little tutorial or something at some point.

960
01:23:21,040 --> 01:23:21,920
That's definitely interesting.

961
01:23:22,000 --> 01:23:23,440
It just feels like a lot of things to do.

962
01:23:24,320 --> 01:23:32,080
Yeah, it feels like especially having spent time at Apple, I was like, that's not the way that Apple would build a product and, you know, that's not the way that I want to build a product.

963
01:23:32,320 --> 01:23:33,520
But when, when you have to.

964
01:23:33,840 --> 01:23:35,280
But Apple's goals are different, right?

965
01:23:35,520 --> 01:23:36,720
It's free, it's built in.

966
01:23:37,120 --> 01:23:39,200
Totally, they're not trying to make money from it, right?

967
01:23:39,440 --> 01:23:39,520
Yeah.

968
01:23:40,400 --> 01:23:48,480
They're trying to just have to be very lightweight and just simple, and that's their, that's their goal, is to be as initially accessible as possible.

969
01:23:48,800 --> 01:23:50,320
And they succeed very well at that.

970
01:23:50,880 --> 01:23:55,760
You know, so it, but it is different when you're an indie maker trying to actually charge money for, for things.

971
01:23:56,080 --> 01:24:02,400
But yeah, you know, the other thing that really struck me, so as a developer, my impression was.

972
01:24:02,400 --> 01:24:09,600
Impression going into the pricing question was that there would be a lot of best practices and that with enough research I would find the answer.

973
01:24:10,800 --> 01:24:13,040
And that is just so not true.

974
01:24:13,280 --> 01:24:18,640
You cannot research your way to an answer for what is the correct pricing model for your app.

975
01:24:19,360 --> 01:24:43,200
There is no other way than to just get dirty, survey and try things and experiment because it's this mix of your space, your app, how good it is, what kind of users you've found, like it's just this mix of all of those and there's just no one answer., you know, fits all solution for a lot of it.

976
01:24:43,440 --> 01:24:53,120
I mean, for a lot of apps, like little utility apps or certain types of apps, like that traditional onetime payment is still probably the right one from a business standpoint.

977
01:24:54,080 --> 01:24:55,600
It really depends a lot on the app.

978
01:24:55,840 --> 01:25:00,000
There's no one size fits all, and I think it took me a long time to accept that, honestly.

979
01:25:00,320 --> 01:25:07,120
I read every single article I could find about pricing for consumer apps.

980
01:25:07,280 --> 01:25:13,120
I bought books on pricing and I just drove myself nuts for months.

981
01:25:13,200 --> 01:25:15,120
I drove everyone that knew me nuts.

982
01:25:19,840 --> 01:25:28,960
Yeah, it's frustrating, especially as an Apple developer designer because so much of, you know, from the beginning of the Mac, right?

983
01:25:29,200 --> 01:25:30,640
The Higg being like, right.

984
01:25:31,280 --> 01:25:36,560
This, it's not, it's not even like, this is the scientifically best way.

985
01:25:36,720 --> 01:25:39,360
It's more like, this is the platform best way.

986
01:25:39,520 --> 01:25:41,360
We're, we're choosing as a platform.

987
01:25:42,400 --> 01:25:43,760
There's a right answer for this.

988
01:25:44,400 --> 01:25:48,560
